      "original_classification": "vulnerability",
      "adjusted_severity": "Informational",
      "reviewer_notes": "This is a deliberate trust boundary design, not a vulnerability. The self-peer bypass at L37-38 is an intentional pattern replicated across ALL gossip validators in Prysm (validateBlob, validateBeaconBlocks, validateAggregateAndProof, validateVoluntaryExit, etc.). When a node publishes via libp2p gossipsub's topicHandle.Publish(), the validator function is invoked on the publishing node with pid=self. The bypass trusts self-published messages because they originate from the node's own validator client and beacon logic, not from untrusted peers. This is a standard libp2p gossipsub pattern: local trust vs. network distrust. The spec's 12 REJECT checks apply to PEER messages (untrusted input); self-published messages are already validated by the publishing code path before reaching gossipsub. Attack scenario assumes malicious local validator, but this is out-of-scope per BUG_BOUNTY_SCOPE.json trust_assumptions.local_validator (trust_level: SEMI_TRUSTED, in_scope_for_bug_bounty: false). Spec cross-reference: SG-006 invariant INV-001 requires all REJECT checks before forwarding to PEERS, not before self-acceptance.",
      "spec_reference": "Fulu p2p-interface SG-006 (data_column_sidecar_gossip_validation): INV-001 requires REJECT checks before forwarding to peers. Self-published messages bypass validation by design (libp2p gossipsub architecture + trust boundary between local code and network peers)."
